00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
tornadoes and hailstorms are some of the  most damaging natural disasters in the  world  thanks to tools like modern weather  radar and a new lightning mapper weather  forecasters can give advance notice for  people to take cover  but what if there was a way to give even  more warning  help may come from satellites  in 2018 researchers published a paper  showing a connection between severe  storms and cloud plumes that flow like  smoke out of them they compared hundreds  of plume storms with radar lightning  data and severe weather warnings  storms that were strong enough to make  these features called above anvil cirrus  plumes or aacps were also more likely to  have severe tornadoes large hail and  high winds  they often appeared 10 to 30 minutes  before severe conditions on the ground  spotting an aacp could save lives by  giving people more time to duck and  cover it could especially help in places  without radar  until now researchers haven't fully  understood how the plumes form  but a new study provides some insight  into that question  scientists simulated an aacp using a  numerical model and environmental  conditions known to produce severe  storms  typically storms stay in the troposphere  the lowest part of the atmosphere where  we live when these storm clouds rise and  reach the stratosphere they often  flatten out creating an anvil-shaped  cloud  in stronger storms the air rises much  faster and higher  the clouds punch their way into the  stratosphere and create features called  overshooting tops  aacps are the wispy cirrus clouds that  form near the overshooting tops  in the new study researchers examined  how these plumes formed when fast  stratospheric winds collided with an  overshooting top  the winds flowed up over the top and  then plunged downwind so fast they  caused something called a hydraulic jump  hydraulic jumps are easy to see and  features such as dams or rivers  when water flows too quickly the flow  will abruptly change  the hydraulic jump is where smooth flow  becomes turbulent  they're also common on the backsides of  mountains fast winds flowing down  mountainsides often leap upwards when  they hit a barrier to their flow  that's why a plane landing near a  mountain might take you on a bumpy ride  curiously the new model showed the  storms acting like a solid mountain or  river rock even though they're made of  fluid  the stratospheric winds had to move  around the storm  in the future the scientists plan to  study the role aacps play in climate  change  according to the model plumes can shoot  more than 7 metric tons of water per  second into the stratosphere  since water vapor acts as a powerful  greenhouse gas in this layer  the plumes could warm the lower  atmosphere even more  creating a cycle of rising temperatures  if the researchers can use tools such as  radar and lidar to confirm their model  our understanding of severe weather and  its climate impacts could take a big  leap forward  [Music]
 

